{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_64","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='run_trace/galaxy_tool_schema_005/build_response.json'\nd=json.load(open(p))\nprint(d.get('help'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"<p><strong>What it does</strong></p>\n<p>This tool joins two tabular files based on a common key column.</p>\n<hr class=\"docutils\" />\n<p><strong>Example</strong></p>\n<p><strong>First file</strong>:</p>\n<pre class=\"literal-block\">\nFruit       Color\nApple       red\nBanana      yellow\nOrange      orange\nMelon       green\n</pre>\n<p><strong>Second File</strong>:</p>\n<pre class=\"literal-block\">\nFruit       Price\nOrange      7\nAvocado     8\nApple       4\nBanana      3\n</pre>\n<p><strong>Joining</strong> both files, using <strong>key column 1</strong> and a <strong>header line</strong>, will return:</p>\n<pre class=\"literal-block\">\nFruit       Color   Price\nApple       red     4\nAvocado     .       8\nBanana      yellow  3\nMelon       green   .\nOrange      orange  7\n</pre>\n<ul class=\"infomark simple\">\n<li>Input files need not be sorted.</li>\n<li>The header line (<strong>Fruit  Color  Price</strong>) was joined and kept as first line.</li>\n<li>Missing values ( Avocado's color, missing from the first file ) are replaced with a period character.</li>\n</ul>\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
